The terminal across clients, and its viewport across resizes.

Cross-client sync. A terminal belongs to the box, not to the browser tab that
opened it. Workspace identity is canonicalised from the folder path, so the same
workspace reached through a different origin (localhost against a LAN address)
is one workspace; layouts merge across clients instead of the last writer
winning; attach state is per client and only the final detach releases the pty.
One attached client is the controller and only its resizes reach the pty host,
so a phone toggling its keyboard cannot resize a desktop viewer's shell - taking
control hands the new controller its own cached dimensions. Replays are bound to
the renderer that asked for them, so concurrent starts do not cross wires.

Resize scroll. xterm's Buffer#resize advances ydisp once per row it drops off
the top, wherever the viewport happened to be. That is right while the viewport
follows the bottom - the cursor has to stay visible, so the content moves up by
exactly the height that was taken away - and wrong once the user has scrolled up
to read, where it drags the text out from under them by that same height.
Nothing puts it back, so this does. A soft keyboard shrinking the panel is the
cheapest way to see it, a sash drag does it on desktop just as well, so it is
not gated on the touch capability. Columns are not part of it: Buffer#_reflow
returns immediately unless the column count changed. Delete it the day xterm
anchors the viewport itself.

